1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,960
At seven in the morning, the
blinds in your apartment open to

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:07,840
sunlight. Beyond the window
is a neighbourhood of trees,

3
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:11,000
balconies, and people walking to work.

4
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:13,800
Then you look up.

5
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:18,540
High above the park, the opposite
side of your city curves

6
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:22,160
across the sky. Other
streets hang overhead,

7
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:27,460
kilometres away. And through a
narrow observation gallery, you

8
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:31,280
can see Earth. Silent and brilliant

9
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:35,760
against black space. You are not on another

10
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:40,260
planet. You are living inside
an enormous structure built

11
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:44,400
in orbit. This is the first city

12
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,140
in space. There is no such city today.

13
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:52,480
What you're seeing is a
scientifically informed future

14
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:56,140
concept, not a project
secretly under construction.

15
00:00:56,140 --> 00:01:00,320
But the underlying idea
is surprisingly old.

16
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:04,460
Engineers have studied rotating
settlements for decades.

17
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,640
The question is not whether
we can draw a beautiful

18
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:12,280
spacecraft. It's whether we
could build a place where

19
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:16,540
thousands of people might
wake up, raise families, grow

20
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:20,900
food, and live ordinary
lives without the ground of a

21
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:25,440
planet beneath them. The
first challenge is one you

22
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,200
feel every second.

23
00:01:31,389 --> 00:01:33,949
Stand inside a normal
spacecraft and you float.

24
00:01:35,069 --> 00:01:37,749
It's not because Earth has
stopped pulling on you.

25
00:01:38,689 --> 00:01:41,809
The spacecraft and its occupants
are falling around Earth

26
00:01:41,809 --> 00:01:46,029
together. Now imagine a
huge ring slowly turning.

27
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:49,009
People stand on the
inside of its outer wall.

28
00:01:49,809 --> 00:01:53,409
As the floor carries them in a
circle, it continually pushes

29
00:01:53,409 --> 00:01:57,329
them inward. And they experience
something like weight.

30
00:01:58,149 --> 00:02:01,209
That's artificial gravity through rotation.

31
00:02:02,749 --> 00:02:04,849
It isn't a mysterious gravity generator.

32
00:02:05,769 --> 00:02:07,769
It is a consequence of circular motion.

33
00:02:08,829 --> 00:02:12,969
But size matters. Spin a tiny
room quickly enough to make its

34
00:02:12,969 --> 00:02:14,249
floor feel like Earth.

35
00:02:14,969 --> 00:02:17,929
And moving your head or
walking around could produce

36
00:02:17,929 --> 00:02:22,509
uncomfortable sensations. Large
rotating habitats can achieve

37
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:25,249
the same acceleration with
much slower rotation.

38
00:02:26,509 --> 00:02:30,789
A classic Stanford Torus design
from a NASA-sponsored study

39
00:02:30,789 --> 00:02:34,929
in the 1970s, imagined a
ring roughly a mile across,

40
00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:36,989
turning about once per minute.

41
00:02:37,809 --> 00:02:41,009
A city on its inner rim could
feel surprisingly familiar.

42
00:02:41,869 --> 00:02:45,089
Move toward the central axis
however, and the apparent gravity

43
00:02:45,089 --> 00:02:50,849
would fade. So what

44
00:02:50,849 --> 00:02:53,029
shape should the very first city take?

45
00:02:55,504 --> 00:02:59,844
Picture three possibilities. The first is a

46
00:02:59,844 --> 00:03:03,864
giant wheel. Homes and
gardens follow the circular

47
00:03:03,864 --> 00:03:07,764
rim. Spokes connect the
neighbourhoods to a central hub

48
00:03:07,764 --> 00:03:12,084
where visiting spacecraft can
arrive without trying to land

49
00:03:12,084 --> 00:03:13,864
on a fast-moving street.

50
00:03:16,184 --> 00:03:18,284
The second is a long cylinder.

51
00:03:20,064 --> 00:03:22,884
Its inside wall becomes a curved landscape.

52
00:03:23,924 --> 00:03:27,324
From one village, you could
look straight across the open

53
00:03:27,324 --> 00:03:31,044
interior and see farms and
houses above your head.

54
00:03:33,914 --> 00:03:38,014
The third is a collection of
smaller modules linked to a

55
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:42,474
rotating structure. It would
be easier to expand in stages,

56
00:03:43,474 --> 00:03:47,614
but a growing network introduces
more joints, seals, and

57
00:03:47,614 --> 00:03:51,954
moving parts. None of these

58
00:03:51,954 --> 00:03:55,274
designs is a finished city
waiting to be ordered.

59
00:03:56,794 --> 00:04:00,874
They are engineering
trade-offs, mass, structural

60
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:05,134
forces, maintenance,
radiation shielding, human

61
00:04:05,134 --> 00:04:07,454
comfort, and construction cost.

62
00:04:09,034 --> 00:04:11,834
A rotating habitat must
be balanced carefully.

63
00:04:13,394 --> 00:04:17,934
Streets, water tanks, and even
new buildings become part of

64
00:04:17,934 --> 00:04:21,094
a moving system whose
dynamics must be controlled.

65
00:04:22,094 --> 00:04:25,674
The interior may resemble a garden.

66
00:04:26,874 --> 00:04:31,034
Outside, every beam is part
of an extraordinary machine.

67
00:04:33,792 --> 00:04:36,872
Before there are apartments,
there is construction.

68
00:04:38,112 --> 00:04:41,932
Imagine autonomous robotic
workers positioning metal frames

69
00:04:41,932 --> 00:04:43,772
against the blackness of space.

70
00:04:44,912 --> 00:04:47,512
Cargo vehicles deliver structural parts.

71
00:04:48,312 --> 00:04:52,712
Welding, inspection, and assembly
happen in a vacuum, with

72
00:04:52,712 --> 00:04:54,952
no convenient ground beneath the machines.

73
00:04:55,912 --> 00:04:59,272
A conventional approach would
launch countless pieces from

74
00:04:59,272 --> 00:05:01,132
Earth and connect them in orbit.

75
00:05:01,132 --> 00:05:05,472
That means extraordinary
transportation demand, even

76
00:05:05,472 --> 00:05:08,912
before adding water, shielding,
and the first residents.

77
00:05:10,332 --> 00:05:13,652
Another approach is to use
material obtained beyond Earth,

78
00:05:14,512 --> 00:05:18,932
perhaps lunar rock for shielding,
or metal extracted and

79
00:05:18,932 --> 00:05:20,452
processed from space resources.

80
00:05:21,672 --> 00:05:25,632
Those ideas could reduce some
launch requirements, but mining,

81
00:05:26,012 --> 00:05:29,012
refining, and transporting
material in space would themselves

82
00:05:29,012 --> 00:05:32,852
need industries that do not yet
exist at the required scale.

83
00:05:33,652 --> 00:05:37,532
NASA has also explored the
possibility of expandable kilometre

84
00:05:37,532 --> 00:05:40,212
scale structures that
could unfold after launch.

85
00:05:41,172 --> 00:05:45,232
That remains research, not proof
that a city can pop open like

86
00:05:45,232 --> 00:05:49,092
a camping tent. Before
the wheel begins turning,

87
00:05:49,772 --> 00:05:52,872
thousands of components must work as one.

88
00:05:54,092 --> 00:05:58,552
Every attachment matters.
Every repair procedure

89
00:05:58,552 --> 00:06:02,612
needs a backup. And even if
the structure is perfect,

90
00:06:03,132 --> 00:06:07,132
space has a danger that no city
on Earth can simply ignore.

91
00:06:09,547 --> 00:06:12,647
From the outside, a city and
space might look delicate.

92
00:06:13,707 --> 00:06:17,567
In reality, its walls could need
to be enormously substantial.

93
00:06:18,747 --> 00:06:22,407
Beyond Earth's atmosphere, people
face high-energy radiation

94
00:06:22,407 --> 00:06:25,927
from space and occasionally
from violent solar events.

95
00:06:26,887 --> 00:06:30,367
Long-term exposure is a major
concern for any settlement.

96
00:06:31,547 --> 00:06:35,707
One proposed solution is
mass, water, specially

97
00:06:35,707 --> 00:06:39,587
selected materials, or thick
layers of rock placed around

98
00:06:39,587 --> 00:06:43,827
inhabited sections. That means
the famous science fiction

99
00:06:43,827 --> 00:06:48,307
city beneath a transparent glass
bubble is not necessarily a

100
00:06:48,307 --> 00:06:51,887
sensible design. You would want controlled,

101
00:06:52,687 --> 00:06:56,207
shielded areas with carefully
engineered places to look

102
00:06:56,207 --> 00:07:00,607
outside. Small debris and micrometeoroids

103
00:07:00,607 --> 00:07:04,867
present another risk.
Pressure boundaries, impact

104
00:07:04,867 --> 00:07:09,187
protection, compartment doors,
and reliable leak detection

105
00:07:09,187 --> 00:07:11,587
would matter every day.

106
00:07:13,607 --> 00:07:16,987
A puncture would not mean the
entire city instantly explodes,

107
00:07:17,887 --> 00:07:22,147
but a serious leak could be
lethal without fast isolation and

108
00:07:22,147 --> 00:07:26,687
repairs. Fire behaves
differently in controlled

109
00:07:26,687 --> 00:07:30,987
spacecraft environments,
too. Ventilation and safety

110
00:07:30,987 --> 00:07:33,347
systems become part of every building.

111
00:07:35,817 --> 00:07:37,577
The city is not simply architecture.

112
00:07:40,447 --> 00:07:43,067
It is a machine that must
protect its inhabitants

113
00:07:43,067 --> 00:07:48,307
continuously, without
ever taking a day off.

114
00:07:50,817 --> 00:07:55,157
A terrestrial city turns
beneath the sun and receives

115
00:07:55,157 --> 00:07:58,617
a natural rhythm of morning and night.

116
00:08:00,487 --> 00:08:04,197
Inside an orbital settlement,
the design team would have

117
00:08:04,197 --> 00:08:08,297
choices. Large mirrors
might redirect sunlight

118
00:08:08,297 --> 00:08:10,917
into selected interior regions.

119
00:08:11,897 --> 00:08:15,037
Shutters and reflectors could
create a planned day-night

120
00:08:15,037 --> 00:08:18,537
cycle, helping people
maintain familiar routines.

121
00:08:19,537 --> 00:08:23,737
Other areas might rely on
efficient artificial illumination,

122
00:08:24,617 --> 00:08:28,817
especially for agriculture. Solar

123
00:08:28,817 --> 00:08:32,497
arrays would be an obvious
power source in many orbital

124
00:08:32,497 --> 00:08:35,917
locations. But a city would need storage,

125
00:08:36,557 --> 00:08:40,957
distribution networks, and
redundant systems for times when

126
00:08:40,957 --> 00:08:43,957
sunlight was unavailable
or equipment failed.

127
00:08:47,307 --> 00:08:51,487
Imagine a school courtyard
lit by a warm simulated

128
00:08:51,487 --> 00:08:55,607
sunrise, while technicians
outside repair a mirror panel

129
00:08:55,607 --> 00:09:00,027
against the dark. Now
imagine switching from a

130
00:09:00,027 --> 00:09:03,767
residential garden to an industrial
workshop near the central

131
00:09:03,767 --> 00:09:07,587
hub, where near weightlessness
could be useful for certain

132
00:09:07,587 --> 00:09:09,687
kinds of manufacturing and handling.

133
00:09:10,627 --> 00:09:14,727
That is the unusual promise
of a rotating settlement,

134
00:09:15,067 --> 00:09:19,567
multiple environments inside
the same built world.

135
00:09:20,227 --> 00:09:23,807
Yet light is only one ingredient.

136
00:09:23,807 --> 00:09:27,447
A city also has to feed itself,

137
00:09:28,147 --> 00:09:30,047
breathe, and drink.

138
00:09:32,488 --> 00:09:36,288
On Earth, a broken water pipe is serious.

139
00:09:37,768 --> 00:09:41,148
In a sealed space settlement,
losing air or wasting water

140
00:09:41,148 --> 00:09:43,008
could endanger an entire neighbourhood.

141
00:09:43,828 --> 00:09:47,448
Life support would need to
circulate and clean air, recover

142
00:09:47,448 --> 00:09:51,668
water, monitor contaminants,
and handle waste continuously.

143
00:09:53,248 --> 00:09:56,928
Modern spacecraft already use
sophisticated recycling systems,

144
00:09:57,588 --> 00:10:01,288
but 1,000-person city is a very
different challenge from a

145
00:10:01,288 --> 00:10:02,748
small space station crew.

146
00:10:03,828 --> 00:10:06,668
Greenhouses could produce
food and contribute to the

147
00:10:06,668 --> 00:10:10,468
environment, with carefully
controlled humidity, nutrients,

148
00:10:11,028 --> 00:10:12,748
light, and pest management.

149
00:10:13,568 --> 00:10:17,528
They would also require energy,
spare parts, expertise, and

150
00:10:17,528 --> 00:10:19,128
reserves when harvests failed.

151
00:10:20,168 --> 00:10:23,348
A self-sufficient ecosystem
would be an astonishing

152
00:10:23,348 --> 00:10:27,288
engineering achievement. Calling
it closed-loop would not make

153
00:10:27,288 --> 00:10:29,648
it magically independent
of outside supplies.

154
00:10:30,868 --> 00:10:34,388
Some goods would almost certainly
arrive on ships, at least in

155
00:10:34,388 --> 00:10:37,668
the early decades, medicines,
specialist electronics,

156
00:10:38,268 --> 00:10:41,248
replacement machinery, and
materials that were difficult to

157
00:10:41,248 --> 00:10:45,768
manufacture locally. The most
important facility might not be

158
00:10:45,768 --> 00:10:48,268
the tallest tower, or the largest park.

159
00:10:48,268 --> 00:10:52,448
It might be the unglamorous
room that keeps the water

160
00:10:52,448 --> 00:10:55,268
clean, while everyone else sleeps.

161
00:10:58,039 --> 00:10:59,759
what would life actually feel like?

162
00:11:01,059 --> 00:11:04,879
At the outer rim, you could walk
to a café and watch cyclists

163
00:11:04,879 --> 00:11:07,219
travel along a curved avenue.

164
00:11:08,499 --> 00:11:11,779
Children might play beneath a
ceiling that is really the far

165
00:11:11,779 --> 00:11:13,599
side of their own neighbourhood.

166
00:11:15,079 --> 00:11:18,759
A lift could carry you inward
toward the rotation axis,

167
00:11:19,479 --> 00:11:21,659
where the sensation of
weight becomes weaker.

168
00:11:22,659 --> 00:11:26,939
Near the hub, cargo handlers
might float as they move supplies

169
00:11:26,939 --> 00:11:29,119
between docking craft and the spokes.

170
00:11:32,479 --> 00:11:33,719
You would learn new instincts.

171
00:11:34,839 --> 00:11:38,099
A thrown ball would not trace
quite the same path you expect

172
00:11:38,099 --> 00:11:42,559
on Earth. Running in the
direction of the spin could

173
00:11:42,559 --> 00:11:44,679
feel different from running against it.

174
00:11:45,619 --> 00:11:50,099
Most importantly, you would
not spend every moment staring

175
00:11:50,099 --> 00:11:53,559
at the stars. You would go to work,

176
00:11:54,199 --> 00:11:58,559
argue over dinner, wait for the train, grow

177
00:11:58,559 --> 00:12:01,199
old, and make plans for the weekend.

178
00:12:04,219 --> 00:12:07,359
A functioning city needs
more than breathable air and

179
00:12:07,359 --> 00:12:11,359
artificial gravity. It needs laws, schools,

180
00:12:12,079 --> 00:12:16,399
healthcare, trust,
culture, and the right to

181
00:12:16,399 --> 00:12:18,059
disagree about how it is run.

182
00:12:21,819 --> 00:12:24,499
Building a settlement is
not just building hardware.

183
00:12:24,499 --> 00:12:28,759
It is building a society.

184
00:12:31,238 --> 00:12:33,918
Could humanity really
build a city in space?

185
00:12:35,438 --> 00:12:38,578
There is nothing in the laws
of physics that rules out a

186
00:12:38,578 --> 00:12:42,998
rotating habitat. A spinning
structure can create gravity-like

187
00:12:42,998 --> 00:12:44,658
acceleration for its residents.

188
00:12:45,618 --> 00:12:46,958
Water can be recycled.

189
00:12:48,098 --> 00:12:50,598
Plants can grow in controlled environments.

190
00:12:52,018 --> 00:12:54,998
And robotic construction is
already an active field of

191
00:12:54,998 --> 00:12:59,578
research. But demonstrating
a component is not the same

192
00:12:59,578 --> 00:13:03,658
as delivering a safe, affordable
settlement for thousands of

193
00:13:03,658 --> 00:13:07,818
people. We would need reliable
construction at enormous

194
00:13:07,818 --> 00:13:12,318
scale, practical radiation
protection, long-duration medical

195
00:13:12,318 --> 00:13:16,278
evidence, industrial supply
chains, and economic reasons to

196
00:13:16,278 --> 00:13:20,758
sustain the place. There
are also questions nobody

197
00:13:20,758 --> 00:13:22,758
can answer with a beautiful rendering.

198
00:13:23,418 --> 00:13:24,878
Who owns the structure?

199
00:13:25,798 --> 00:13:27,218
Who pays for an emergency?

200
00:13:28,418 --> 00:13:29,538
Who gets to live there?

201
00:13:30,218 --> 00:13:32,638
And who decides what happens
when something breaks?

202
00:13:34,158 --> 00:13:38,698
Perhaps our first true space
city would look nothing like

203
00:13:38,698 --> 00:13:41,238
the grand paintings of the 1970s.

204
00:13:42,238 --> 00:13:45,718
Perhaps it would begin with
a handful of modules and grow

205
00:13:45,718 --> 00:13:50,258
slowly over generations.
But imagine the first

206
00:13:50,258 --> 00:13:54,338
child born there, looking
upward at a curved sky,

207
00:13:54,338 --> 00:13:58,118
full of other people's homes,
and knowing that the entire

208
00:13:58,118 --> 00:14:00,318
neighbourhood was made by human hands.

209
00:14:01,478 --> 00:14:05,218
The first city in space would
not be an escape from Earth.

210
00:14:06,538 --> 00:14:09,678
It would be proof that the
idea of home can travel.

